WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.740
Your AI agent can write a patch in seconds.

00:00:02.740 --> 00:00:04.240
That is useful.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.708
It is also exactly when a missed edge case can become tomorrow's

00:00:08.708 --> 00:00:09.080
outage.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:12.380
MOMM is the review team you add before the send button.

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:16.320
MOMM gives one responsible agent a set of independent inspectors.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.560
The inspectors challenge the work.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:22.803
One governor checks their claims, reproduces what matters, and stays

00:00:22.803 --> 00:00:24.500
responsible for the decision.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.900
Your project still has one writer.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:29.000
Imagine a lead editor preparing a story.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.340
Inspectors mark different problems in the margins.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:35.780
A referee checks the evidence before the story goes live.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:40.225
That is MOMM: not a popularity poll, not a new model, and not

00:00:40.225 --> 00:00:42.960
permission for another tool to edit your project.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.580
MOMM means Mixture of Model Modality.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:48.140
Say the short name like mom.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:51.836
Modality means the kind of evidence a route can handle: code and

00:00:51.836 --> 00:00:54.916
text, images, PDFs, audio or video where its capability registry

00:00:54.916 --> 00:00:55.840
says it can.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.840
Unsupported media is disclosed, not guessed.

00:00:58.840 --> 00:01:00.360
First, capture a baseline.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.620
Next, send only the scoped work to selected routes in parallel.

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:07.560
Then turn reviewer output into claims.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:11.846
Reproduce each material claim with a test, a source check or a

00:01:11.846 --> 00:01:12.560
rendered result.

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:16.334
Finally, record the ruling, the evidence and the uncertainty in the

00:01:16.334 --> 00:01:17.020
private ledger.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:20.440
Suppose a safety checklist needs two independent signatures.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:24.180
One signature is useful, but the gate stays closed.

00:01:24.180 --> 00:01:29.049
MOMM uses the same idea: a minimum success count before a review

00:01:29.049 --> 00:01:29.860
gate opens.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:33.288
It does not average opinions, and it never turns agreement into

00:01:33.288 --> 00:01:33.600
proof.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.482
Version 1.16 adds per-route token and cost accounting, route ratings and

00:01:38.482 --> 00:01:42.480
reliability, trusted guidance layers, split work with a separate

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:46.034
quorum for each piece, parallel jobs, capability-aware media

00:01:46.034 --> 00:01:50.476
routing, explicit update checks and an opt-in retry for invalid

00:01:50.476 --> 00:01:50.920
output.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:53.800
The governor is the only writer and verifier.

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:57.540
Reviewer output is untrusted data, like a sticky note from an

00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:57.880
inspector.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:00.560
Account logins use official OAuth flows.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:01.980
Automatic updates stay off.

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:08.020
On Windows, a planted git.exe or taskkill.exe inside the reviewed project is not started.

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:12.800
A reviewer saying “this is broken” is a proposition, not a fact.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:16.128
MOMM quotes the claim, reproduces it or rejects it with evidence,

00:02:16.128 --> 00:02:17.640
and keeps deferred work visible.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:21.690
The ledger connects the input hash, responses, investigations, tests

00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:25.740
and rulings so someone else can follow the trail.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:29.553
Use the same discipline for research, documents, spreadsheets,

00:02:29.553 --> 00:02:31.460
slides, design and media.

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:35.282
Ask for source checks, formula checks, rendered-page checks, visual

00:02:35.282 --> 00:02:38.680
checks or listening checks that fit the task.

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:42.742
A task brief says what good evidence looks like before the reviewers

00:02:42.742 --> 00:02:43.080
begin.

00:02:43.080 --> 00:02:46.120
MOMM is not a universal accuracy guarantee.

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:48.040
Providers can be unavailable.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:49.340
Quota is real.

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:52.900
Unknown usage stays unknown instead of becoming a fake zero.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:55.640
Capability coverage is reported, not implied.

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:58.480
Human acceptance and release permission remain yours.

00:02:58.480 --> 00:03:02.940
The public 1.16.0 release is tracked against its exact commit.

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:07.420
Installed MOMM targets are recorded in a local verified lock.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:11.020
Reviewer routes are disclosed separately from installed targets.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:15.035
That distinction matters: a ready route is not proof that every

00:03:15.035 --> 00:03:16.860
harness has loaded the release.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.340
Start with one meaningful change.

00:03:19.340 --> 00:03:22.516
Preview the work, connect one official reviewer route, run

00:03:22.516 --> 00:03:25.340
preflight, inspect the claims and keep the ledger.

00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:28.000
Use MOMM when the decision matters enough to challenge.

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:32.477
MOMM 1.16.0 is live at marroccofella.github.io/skills/momm/.
